# Shell scripts are run straight out of the working tree by a Linux container
# (`just ci-linux`), so they have to keep their line endings whatever the host
# checks out with. `sh` reads a carriage return as part of the command and fails
# on the first line it meets one.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Frost sources are what the formatter writes, and it writes line feeds. A
# checkout that rewrites them to carriage returns makes every file differ from
# its own formatting, so `the_corpus_is_formatted` fails on a tree nobody
# edited. Said outright rather than left to the host's `core.autocrlf`.
*.frost text eol=lf

# The same for everything else the tree's own tools write: `cargo fmt` writes
# line feeds, so does the book, so does the runtime. A checkout that rewrites
# them makes every file differ from what its formatter would produce.
*.rs text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.c text eol=lf
*.h text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf

# The two runtime libraries the graphics examples load, in the tree so a
# checkout builds without fetching anything. Said outright rather than left to
# content sniffing, since a line ending rewritten inside one is a library that
# will not load.
*.dll binary
*.lib binary
*.a binary
